00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.540
The economic system is a solution to scarcity.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.170
In fact, money, as a system, is a solution
to scarcity.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:23.050
Fundamentally, the world does not have enough
of what people want so we’ve created this

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.430
system to allocate things.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.390
If everyone in the world were offered a pineapple,
free of charge, nearly everyone would probably

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.220
take that pineapple.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.530
If everyone in the world were offered any
resource, in fact, they would probably take

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.120
that resource.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:44.070
The problem is, the world doesn’t have enough
pineapples—there’s a limited supply.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.820
There aren’t enough pineapples for everyone,
so the market economy, at least in theory,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:54.060
matches them to those that want them most—those
that will give up enough money to get that

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.060
pineapple.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.969
Simultaneously, though, this system motivates
people to produce more of what is scarce since

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:01.469
they get paid.

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:06.799
At a fundamental level, whenever something
needs to be allocated, it is monetized.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.150
This system does, however, have its flaws.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.310
How much someone is willing to pay isn’t
perfectly correlated to how much they need

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.850
or want a thing.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.939
In the purest form of this system, every participant
would have the same amount of money and the

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:25.729
same ability to pay so that what they would
offer for a scarce resource would perfectly

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:30.869
correlate to how much they want it, but that,
of course, is not the case.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:37.329
In the real world, some people have more money
than others and so this system breaks down.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:41.799
One poor person might really want a pineapple
and offer a full percent of their money to

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:46.890
get it, but a rich person with eleven times
the money might sort of want a pineapple and

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.779
offer 1/10th of a percent of their money to
get it, and they will get the pineapple.

00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:56.409
In this circumstance, the market system breaks
down as the person who wanted the pineapple

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:58.590
less has received it.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.600
The overall social good is not maximized.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.859
Collectively, though, we have decided that
perfect is the enemy of good and that a certain

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:11.680
level of inequality is ok, especially as,
according to some, it motivates people to

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.690
create more value.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.750
Now, inequality in the pineapple market is
somewhat unfortunate, but ultimately not a

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.220
huge deal.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.220
In other markets, though, human ethics have
decided that a monetized market system, with

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.970
that one, fundamental flaw of inequality,
would be wrong.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.891
When the allocation of a scarce resource means
the difference between life or death, when

00:02:34.891 --> 00:02:41.050
whether a person gets something is that important,
a market system’s inequality is perpetuated

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.920
because people’s willingness to pay is infinite,
it’s not proportional to how much they need

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.680
or want a thing, and therefore, it will always
go to who has more money.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.050
That is the issue with organs.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:59.500
In this case, allocation needs to happen a
different way.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:05.530
Every nine minutes in the US, a doctor makes
a life-changing conclusion about their patient.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.090
They determine that that individual, and their
condition, has progressed to the point that

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.680
they will not survive without an organ transplant.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.200
As soon as that happens, the individual is
placed into a nationwide system that works

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.640
to balance societal utility and human ethics
to match organs to those that need and deserve

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.210
them most.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:30.780
The core of all issues related to organ transplantation
is the fact that there are more people waiting

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.850
for transplants than there are organs available.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:38.570
Just like pineapples, iPhones, or anything
in the traditional economy, there are less

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:42.430
of it than people want of it if the cost was
zero.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:50.720
In the US, 112,000 people are on those lists,
whereas only 39,718 transplants were actually

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.010
performed in 2019.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.350
This means that patients often have to wait
years for a new organ.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:02.320
The exact time, though, varies dramatically
depending on a number of factors.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.270
Patients with the most severe and urgent form
of liver failure, for example, only have to

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:11.080
wait a median of six days whereas a patient
at a lower priority for a heart transplant

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.550
would wait a median of 726 days.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:19.949
Now, when every name on that list is quite
literally on the precipice between life and

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:23.240
death, fairness is tough.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.150
Each country's transplant lists works slightly
differently, but most work fairly similarly

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.509
to the US’.

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:33.520
There, much of an individual’s position
on the list is based on how much use they’re

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.340
going to get from the organ.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.199
Kidney’s, for example, are the most commonly
transplanted organ out there, and in the US,

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:44.520
the major determinant for those on the waiting
list is their “Estimated Post-Transplant

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.100
Survival” score.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.090
Essentially, one wants to give an organ to
an individual for which it will provide the

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.120
most additional years to their life.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.800
For example, a 30 year-old who has not had
dialysis, does not have diabetes, and has

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:03.210
never had an organ transplant before would
have an EPTS score of 2%, one of the highest,

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:08.099
meaning their chances of surviving ten years
post-transplant are above 90%.

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:12.500
Therefore, they would be at the top of the
list, eligible to receive a kidney from the

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.360
highest-quality tranche—expected to function
better than most other donor kidneys.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:23.419
Meanwhile, a 70 year-old who is on dialysis
and has Type 1 diabetes would gain an EPTS

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:28.610
score of 89%, meaning their 10-year survival
outlook is less than 50%.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:35.129
Therefore, they would only be offered a transplant
after all matches with a greater outlook are.

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:40.419
Within these tranches, based on the EPTS score,
further ranking is done based on other scores

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:41.740
and waiting times.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:48.090
Now, the list itself is certainly not just
thousands of names, ranked from one to 90,000,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.680
waiting their turn for the next available
organ.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.770
It’s an incredibly complex system mixing
factors including geography, compatibility,

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.789
necessity, and more.

00:05:57.789 --> 00:06:02.600
Kidneys themselves are unique among organs
in that the human body has two, and, for the

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.610
most part, individuals only need one.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:10.449
Therefore, an individual can donate one of
theirs and be at only a very slight increased

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:14.309
risk of kidney failure or other complications
long-term.

00:06:14.309 --> 00:06:19.839
That is the living-donor route, but the vast
majority of kidneys still come from deceased

00:06:19.839 --> 00:06:20.839
donors.

00:06:20.839 --> 00:06:26.020
However, while 54% of Americans are registered
as organ donors, individuals have to die in

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.490
a very specific way in order for their organs
to be eligible for donation.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.419
They typically have to either have a terminal
condition where they are removed from life

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:38.810
support in a controlled manner, or suffer
from brain death, where the organs continue

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.780
functioning while all brain function have
ceased.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:46.759
This specificity of mortality contributes
to the scarcity of organs since, even after

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:52.279
one of these scenarios happens, one has to
consider the compatibility, health, and eligibility

00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:54.120
of the donor organs.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.439
It also means that the best organs, those
from young, healthy donors, are even rarer

00:06:59.439 --> 00:07:03.440
since they are less likely to die in such
a controlled manner.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:10.490
So, this system of allocation through a non-monetary
system is designed to appeal to human ethics,

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:16.919
designed to work to match kidneys based on
need, not ability to pay, but it too has its

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:18.069
flaws.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:21.779
The root cause of this is around the way the
surgery works.

00:07:21.779 --> 00:07:27.949
Kidneys can only survive outside the body
for 24 to 36 hours without significant deterioration,

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:32.349
so once one becomes available, surgery has
to happen fast.

00:07:32.349 --> 00:07:37.529
Therefore, for the purpose of matching, the
US is split up into eleven regions, and these

00:07:37.529 --> 00:07:40.419
regions certainly aren’t all created equal.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:46.229
For example, region 3, comprised of Arkansas,
Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama, Georgia,

00:07:46.229 --> 00:07:53.099
and Florida, donated 4,055 kidneys in 2019
whereas region 9, comprised of New York state

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:56.379
and western Vermont, only donated 1,564 in
the same year.

00:07:56.379 --> 00:07:59.820
That’s despite the fact that the population
of region 9 is about 45 million while the

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:00.820
population of region 3 is 20 million.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.309
Now, the root cause behind this disparity
is fairly obvious.

00:08:03.309 --> 00:08:08.240
The states that make up region 3 are part
of what’s known as the “stroke belt.”

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.089
That’s because the population of this region
is far more likely to suffer from a stroke

00:08:13.089 --> 00:08:15.099
than the national average.

00:08:15.099 --> 00:08:19.969
Strokes are a potential cause of death quite
conducive to organ donation as they can often

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:21.719
lead to brain death.

00:08:21.719 --> 00:08:27.430
Now, organs donated in a given region are
first offered to those listed in that region—after

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:31.910
all, the recipients need to arrive at the
hospital in a matter of hours.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:37.380
Therefore, as region 3 has more organs available,
the median waiting time for a kidney, and

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:42.210
many other organ types, is far shorter here
than in region 9.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:47.310
Interestingly, the rules in the US do allow
those that need a transplant to list themselves

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:51.840
in multiple regions, as long as they follow
all the other rules the same as someone living

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.540
in that region.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.210
That means that they still need to go through
all the testing ahead of time at the local

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:01.480
hospital, and then need to report to that
hospital in time once they get the call that

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.400
an organ is available.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:09.170
When Steve Jobs received a liver transplant
in 2009, for example, he did so in Memphis,

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.130
Tennessee—far from where he lived in California.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:16.240
Tennessee is also considered as part of the
stroke belt, and therefore the transplant

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:20.030
region it is in, 11, has shorter wait times.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:24.331
Of course, wealthier people, such as Steve
Jobs, are able to do things like fly in a

00:09:24.331 --> 00:09:29.130
private jet to get to the transplant hospital
in time, and he even bought a house in Memphis

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.170
to stay in during his recovery.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:36.180
It’s no wonder why there is such a strong
positive correlation between patients listing

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.200
in multiple regions and income levels.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.910
This system certainly perpetuates inequality,
but the alternative is that a patient in New

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:48.170
York is punished for the fact that their region
is healthier.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:55.420
So, overall, the organ transplant system does
not solve inequality, but only reduces it.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:00.820
Simultaneously, though, it does not have a
mechanism to solve the issue of scarcity.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:05.590
The traditional market economy solves scarcity
by paying people more money for what is in

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:11.100
high demand, therefore, in theory, bringing
things to an equilibrium, but this system

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:15.110
has no way to to increase supply when demand
is higher.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:21.390
So, the current system is not perfectly ethical,
and it also potentially exacerbates the shortage,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:26.940
so that has led some people to ask, what if
we just used a normal economic market for

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.500
organ allocation?

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.200
That is what happened in Iran.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:36.690
Previously, the waiting list for kidneys in
the country exceeded that of almost any other

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:42.120
nation due to political instability and cultural
stigma against organ donation.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:47.970
However, in 1988, they implemented a system
where recipients could pay donors for their

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:48.970
organs.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:55.030
Typically, the donor receives a fee of around
$4,500, equivalent to more than half a year’s

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.560
salary, in addition to free health insurance
for a year.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:04.240
Now, this isn’t a truly free market—it
is highly regulated and managed—but reports

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.600
indicate that it has had some success at solving
the scarcity problem.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:14.320
In the years after this program was implemented,
organ donations skyrocketed and, apparently,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.180
there is no longer any waiting list in the
country.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:23.120
That is quite a success compared to a country
like the US where thousands die each year

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:28.830
waiting on a transplant, but opponents argue
that the negatives are just too severe.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:33.170
When you commoditize organs, and especially
kidneys, where humans can donate one and live

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:37.500
using the other, you incentivize desperate
individuals to sell theirs.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:42.320
Meanwhile, you make it even harder for poorer
individuals to receive an organ transplant

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.560
as they can be priced out.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:51.390
Overall, an open organ market increases inequality,
but also increases the number of transplants.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:58.520
In the end, that means there are two basic
choices: saving more lives, but in an unequal,

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:04.270
unfair manner, or saving fewer lives, but
assuring a greater sense of fairness.

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:08.970
Most countries have decided that the second
option is better—they are potentially dooming

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:13.500
some to die in the mission of assuring that
what determines what gets you an organ is

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:18.150
how much you need it, not how much you can
pay for it—but there is increasing pressure

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:22.450
in many places to stimulate supply with compensation.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:28.250
This is a situation where no solution is perfect—you
can’t have a perfectly abundant supply,

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:33.630
and you can’t have a perfectly fair system,
but you can get close to fair or close to

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:35.790
abundance.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:40.470
Inequality is just part of a monetized system,
so even though economics is an incredibly

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:45.780
powerful tool for allocation, it must be used
carefully in the cases where the stakes are

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:49.860
truly life and death.

